<h1 id="preface">Preface</h1>

<p>Due to my weak foundational knowledge, what I’ve achieved is limited to some facts I’ve learned and observed, and has not yet been elevated to a theoretical doctrine.</p>

<h2 id="the-lesser-known-the-sunyazhou-theory">The Lesser-Known “The Sunyazhou Theory”</h2>

<p>For a country to develop, it must start with improving productivity. Technology is so far the only powerful tool humanity has discovered to solve productivity problems.
So I divide productivity into three stages:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Liberating productivity</li>
  <li>Improving productivity</li>
  <li>Breaking through productivity</li>
</ol>

<p>I call it <strong><code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">《孙亚洲理论》</code></strong> (The Sunyazhou Theory). No authoritative journal has published it yet.</p>

<h2 id="verifying-the-theory">Verifying the Theory</h2>

<p>The objective fact that this theory proves is that developing countries must go through the following stages of development:</p>

<ol>
  <li>Small-scale manufacturing of usable goods</li>
  <li>Optimizing and improving craftsmanship with assembly-line operations</li>
  <li>Being phased out and replaced by new products</li>
</ol>

<p>So living in the present, whether in school or at work, <strong>technical personnel have a far greater advantage than ordinary personnel, because it validates my theoretical doctrine—engaging in professions that improve productivity.</strong></p>

<p>I hope that years from now, when people bring up this principle, they’ll think: ah, this is the lesser-known “The Sunyazhou Theory.”</p>

<h1 id="summary">Summary</h1>

<p>When going home for the New Year, someone always asks how much I make working in Beijing — that kind of thing. I just briefly expressed my views and, half-jokingly, formed my own theoretical doctrine.</p>
